talking kind of any normal

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.640
application--

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.930
I need to authenticate myself.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.810
I need to say, hi, I'm
Mike, and I need some way

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.610
of actually proving that.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.930
So I can get information
about myself.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.550
And then I need to
upload that photo.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.250
And so we're living
in a Firebase world.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.290
I can use Cloud
storage for Firebase,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.750
and suck that photo
right up off my device,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.870
and drop it in a
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.050
And then we can use the
magic of Cloud Functions.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:42.000
So Cloud Functions are kind
of event driven compute off

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.650
of various services.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.410
So whenever an object is
dropped in my storage bucket,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.120
I can trigger a function.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.010
And then I'm not a
super savvy developer.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.843
I'm just going to call
the Cloud Vision API.

00:03:51.843 --> 00:03:54.590
I'm not going to write my
own OCR or any of that.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.880
I'm just going to let
Cloud Vision OCR it.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.000
And my function will
get me the response,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.340
look for the largest
dollar value on a receipt,

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.533
and then write that
information to Cloud Firestore.

00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:07.950
And then Cloud
Firestore, maybe we

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.415
want to say aggregate over
the total sum of my expenses

00:04:12.415 --> 00:04:13.540
that I can get that number.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.019
So maybe that'll trigger
another Cloud Function.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:18.120
That Cloud Function
will do a sum,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.510
write back to Cloud
Firestore, and then notify me,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.440
the user, in real time of hey,
you've uploaded this expense.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.107
Your sum is now x.

00:04:28.107 --> 00:04:29.690
And normally, you
could build this app

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.030
where your file storage
is public on the internet,

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.520
and your database is readable
or writable by anyone.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.382
And those are obviously
very bad things.

00:04:36.382 --> 00:04:38.090
So let's walk through
how to not do that,

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.490
so how to kind of do
that security as we're

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.640
building the application.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.132
So my tips are structured.

00:04:44.132 --> 00:04:46.090
We're going to talk about
a tip, and then we're

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.230
going to talk about the
mitigation along the way.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.310
So as I mentioned, the
internet is a very scary place.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.475
You should never trust
things on the client.

00:04:53.475 --> 00:04:55.850
Because your clients can look
something like this, right?

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:57.565
If you've built a
web app on Firebase,

00:04:57.565 --> 00:04:59.440
I'm sure you've copied
and pasted this config

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.090
snippet a million times.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.610
It literally has an API
key, the location of all

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:05.218
of your resources, et cetera.

00:05:05.218 --> 00:05:07.510
And if you leave your resources
public on the internet,

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:08.470
someone can do that.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.553
And they can just say, oh,
I'm going to just write

00:05:10.553 --> 00:05:12.290
to your database anywhere.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:16.120
So you really can't trust the
client to do anything securely.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.700
We've provided a number
of tools in Firebase

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.620
that help mitigate these risks.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.010
But I get this all the time.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.170
People are like, oh
my god, this is scary.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.200
This is out in the world?

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.580
Yes, and it's by design.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.160
And it's not a
problem if you follow

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.310
the rest of the tips
in this presentation.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.720
So the first of those is, right,
I just said trust nothing.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.310
But we have to trust
something, right?

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.780
Otherwise, we can't
really do anything.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.890
And so the way that we do that
is via Firebase authentication.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:48.912
And so we're going
to go into a little--

00:05:48.912 --> 00:05:51.370
I guess, we're going to explain
how Firebase authentication

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.470
works, and why we
can trust it, and use

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:57.240
that as the root of trust for
the rest of our application.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.308
So how many people have
signed in via Google?

00:05:59.308 --> 00:06:01.100
I would assume almost
everyone in the room.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:01.910
Great.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.160
If anyone has looked at the
information you get back,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.620
you get back information
like a user ID.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.870
You get a profile picture URL.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:10.790
You get obviously
your Google ID.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:13.070
And you can imagine
a world where you

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.420
want to say talk to Firestore.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:15.962
And you just pass
along those claims.

00:06:15.962 --> 00:06:18.050
You're like, hey, I'm Mike.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.940
And Firestore is going
to be like, wait,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.880
anyone could say that
they're Mike, right?

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.610
That doesn't make sense.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.480
And so identity providers
have taken to providing you

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.480
an access token.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.320
So if you've ever done OAuth
programming or similar,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.970
you get this access token.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.030
And that's this kind
of opaque string

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:36.330
that just represents you.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.410
And we actually can take
that in Firebase Auth.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.480
We can kind of pass
that along and say, hey,

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.880
I've signed in via Google
with this access token.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.450
And Firebase Auth
can say, hey, Google,

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.190
is this actually a
valid access token?

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.340
Has Mike proven that he
is who he claims to be?

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.310
And Google will be like, yep,
I can verify that that access

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:54.990
token is correct.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.700
And Firebase Auth
will say, OK, great.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.130
Mike has signed in
with Firebase Auth.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.333
Let's actually give him a
user ID in Firebase, right?

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:03.750
Because we can
have people sign in

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:07.110
via Google, or Facebook, or
Twitter, or a custom provider.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.360
We kind of need some
standard that we can all

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.730
rely on within the
Firebase products,

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.200
and so we'll give you a UID.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.620
And that also allows you to
do things like a user signs

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.440
in anonymously, and then we can
upgrade them as they sign in

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.210
and merge those accounts.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.650
We'll give them a user ID.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.310
And we will also say, hey, take
this other cryptographically

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.250
signed token.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.300
So if folks are familiar
with a JSON web token,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.210
we give you this JSON
web token that says

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.730
information about that user.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.320
So it looks like this.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.145
It's this long kind of
seemingly opaque string.

00:07:40.145 --> 00:07:41.520
But it has three
important parts.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.522
It has a header, and
that basically says,

00:07:43.522 --> 00:07:44.980
this is the algorithm
that was used

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.997
to sign the token, et cetera.

00:07:46.997 --> 00:07:48.330
And then this is the juicy part.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.670
It has a body with
a bunch of data.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.920
And that data has
all the same kind

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.030
of provider specific
information.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.090
So things like the
name, the email,

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.160
the name of the
application used, and then

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.022
some other custom claims, like
hey, I signed in via Firebase.

00:08:02.022 --> 00:08:02.730
Here's my Google.

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.560
Or I signed in via Google.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.640
And Firebase, here
are my various emails.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.278
And then it has--

00:08:10.278 --> 00:08:11.820
and this is the most
important part--

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.180
it has a signature.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.050
So there is no prior knowledge
of cryptography necessary.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:16.860
Don't worry.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.350
But basically what
happens here is Google

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.780
has a public private key pair.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.750
So one thing is secret,
your private key.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.360
And then we offer a public
key for verification.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.760
And Firebase Auth will kind
of translate that Google

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.789
credential into a
Firebase Auth token

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:34.650
that it signs with
its private key.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:36.990
And so it basically signs
the header and the data

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.880
and puts that in a signature.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.850
And so if someone comes
along, and they change--

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.559
they intercept your token.

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:47.340
And they try and change it, that
signature no longer matches.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.385
When Firestore,
or Cloud Storage,

00:08:49.385 --> 00:08:50.760
or any of those
Google properties

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.440
will try and understand that
token, they'll say, hey,

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.330
something's wrong.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.520
The signature doesn't match.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.440
And so that is kind of
where our root of trust

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.190
comes from, right?

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:00.750
And it would be,
obviously, very bad

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.950
if those private keys leaked.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.700
And so we rotate them, and we
do all of those security things

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.130
so that you are not trying
to build your own identity

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:08.955
provider.

00:09:08.955 --> 00:09:10.080
Don't roll your own crypto.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.890
Don't build your own
identity providers.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.218
There are lots of
people who've done this.

00:09:14.218 --> 00:09:16.510
So we have done the mistakes
so that you don't have to.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.100
And then we fix
them along the way.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.220
So now when I want to go and
query Firestore, I can say,

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.160
hey, here's my JSON web token.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.210
Pass that along.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:28.850
And then Firestore knows how
to validate that and pull

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.760
a bunch of claims out of that.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:32.600
So there you go.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:33.500
It can say awesome.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:34.100
You're Mike.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.270
You're this user ID.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.250
Here are all the
properties associated

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.360
with you, which gets us
to our next part, right?

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.810
So we know who our users are.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.770
And we can verify and trust that
they are who they claim to be.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.980
But there are a couple
of other steps, right?

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.730
We've done our
authentication of just yes,

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.050
you are who you claim to be.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.925
But now there's the
authorization step of can

00:09:53.925 --> 00:09:56.490
you do the thing that
you're asking to do?

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:58.740
And then, because again, we
don't provide you a server

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.810
to do all these things,
we have to say, OK, how

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.080
do you do schema validation?

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.597
So when you upload a file,
it looks like a certain way.

00:10:04.597 --> 00:10:06.930
Or how do you do business
logic to validate that someone

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.340
isn't inputting malicious data?

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.190
And that's where
security rules comes in.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:12.780
So Firebase security
rules kind of

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.870
stand between your
client and the backend

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:19.980
that you're accessing to provide
you those additional features.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.250
And it does so via this thing
called Attribute Based Access

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:23.750
Control.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:26.042
So some of you have probably
heard of Role Based Access

00:10:26.042 --> 00:10:26.610
Control.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.910
And we'll talk about
that a little later.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.327
But Attribute Based
Access Control

00:10:29.327 --> 00:10:31.630
is just, here are a
bunch of properties.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:35.100
So the user ID, the
request that you're making,

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.080
the resource that
you're accessing,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.570
go and derive some
decision based on that.

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:41.910
So we'll walk through that.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.870
Hopefully, some of you
have seen this before,

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:46.390
but if you haven't
not a big deal.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.460
We typically start by
defining a service.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.330
We are writing rules on storage.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.000
And then we offer this
kind of match construct.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.200
And so we say--

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.290
if anyone, for instance,
has used the RESTful

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.498
APIs for Google Cloud Storage,
storage.googleapis.com

00:10:59.498 --> 00:11:02.240
slash b slash the name
of the bucket slash o

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.240
slash the path to
the object, We're

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:05.340
just doing the same thing.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.800
We're saying, OK, let's
match kind of a RESTful path,

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.710
or the resource that
you want to access.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.630
That little squiggly
character is a wildcard.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.700
So in this case, we're
going to say, hey, match

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.710
the bucket in your project.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.480
And similarly, we
can say, OK, let's

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.110
imagine that we're naming the
images that we're uploading

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:25.410
to Cloud Storage for
Firebase, this user, the name

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.780
of your user ID,
expenses, and then

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.410
your expense.jpg or expense.png.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.750
And then I can do
these allow constructs.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.870
So I can say allow a
read or allow a write

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.310
if some condition is true.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:39.810
And this is the
attribute based part

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.225
that I was talking
about earlier.

00:11:41.225 --> 00:11:42.600
Because those
attributes could be

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.570
if the user matches the
user ID that were here

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:47.620
or a number of other things.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.660
So we'll start
with the first one.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:52.740
And we'll say only let a
user read their expenses

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.760
or write their expenses.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.940
So this request.auth.uid,
we're basically

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.550
pulling a field out of
the token and matching it

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.955
to a name of the file.

00:12:01.955 --> 00:12:04.330
And then we can make it a
little more complicated, right?

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.720
We can say, OK, any user can
read only their documents

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.610
or only their expenses.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:11.560
But now we are going
to enforce a few more

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.940
constraints on writes.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:16.930
Because we don't want someone
uploading a 5 gigabyte tar

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:17.500
ball.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:18.875
We want someone
uploading a thing

00:12:18.875 --> 00:12:21.130
that has to be an image
less than a megabyte.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.020
And again, we can pull
in those attributes

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.383
and make decisions
based on that.

00:12:25.383 --> 00:12:27.300
And then lastly, we can
enforce certain pieces

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.150
of business logic.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:30.630
So imagine you don't
want to allow an expense

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:34.260
to be overwritten
because maybe someone is

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:37.330
trying to change it to look
differently after the fact.

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:39.313
They Photoshopped it to
have a different value.

00:12:39.313 --> 00:12:41.730
And so we're going to say we're
going to require that they

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.390
can't overwrite their object.

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.210
So if there's already
an object there,

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.393
we don't let them write it.

00:12:47.393 --> 00:12:49.060
So that's kind of a
very simple example,

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:51.780
but hopefully, it shows you
the power and flexibility

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.270
of these rules.

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.980
OK, so we've uploaded our file.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.990
We've done so because
we authenticated.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.600
We wrote some rules to ensure
that it's the correct thing.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:04.445
Now we trigger our function.

00:13:04.445 --> 00:13:06.570
And so our function could
look something like this.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.370
If you've ever written Cloud
Functions for Firebase,

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:11.870
you pull in this
Firebase Admin library.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:14.550
We also pulled in a Google
Cloud client library for Vision.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:15.050
Right?

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:18.280
We're going to make one call
out to the Vision API to OCR.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.700
We are going to write a little
bit of code that says, hey,

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.550
find the biggest
number on the screen.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:26.367
And then we're going to write
that out to Cloud Firestore.

00:13:26.367 --> 00:13:28.700
And the one thing that I
really wanted to emphasize here

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:32.100
is we're actually
running as an admin.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.520
So this function is kind of
not running as an end user.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.610
It has a higher
level of privilege.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.380
And it's actually running
using a service account, right?

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:42.740
So we signed in our users
with Firebase authentication.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:45.170
But all of our compute
is running as this kind

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.160
of robot service account user.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.130
And that uses Google Cloud IAM.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:53.710
And so, historically, inside
of all of your functions,

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:56.440
all of those functions
run with the editor role.

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:58.690
We kind of have one identity,
and they all get editor.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.990
So that means that if you
screwed up your programming

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:05.320
and maybe there are
cases where someone

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.800
uploaded a source code file, and
your function [INAUDIBLE] it.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:11.830
Potentially, that function could
now create virtual machines.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:15.610
Or it can't change IM roles,
but it can build other resources

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:18.460
or do things that it really
shouldn't be doing, right?

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:21.020
All our function needs is this
concept of least privilege,

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:21.520
right?

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:26.830
It needs to read from cloud
storage to do a vision check

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.260
and then write to
Cloud Firestore.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.140
So we have this concept of
per function identities.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.290
And what that means is you can
create a new service account

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.660
and have that be given just
the amount of privilege

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:38.800
that it needs.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:40.300
And you can do that
via custom roles

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:43.450
in IM or minting access
tokens if you're accessing,

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:46.840
say, the Google Drive
or Google Sheets APIs.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:48.320
So we'll walk through that.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:49.540
So I have a function.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.390
It was called OCR receipt
because that's what it does.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.100
It OCRs a receipt.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.620
And I'm going to create a new
service account to represent

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:59.040
the identity of that function.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:01.170
And then I'm going to
create a custom role,

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.320
and I'm going to call
that role only the Cloud

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.040
Vision in Firestore thing.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:08.280
And I'm going to pass
it two permissions.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:09.930
And this is a little
counterintuitive.

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:13.520
Vision actually needs object get
because we're pulling from GCS.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.020
And then the only
permission that it

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:17.970
needs to right to Firestore is,
again, sort of confusingly data

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:21.720
store entities create,
as a bit of legacy there.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:23.700
But we've now gone
from editor where

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.910
it could do anything in our
project, module a few things,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.360
to exactly two permissions.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.450
And then we are going to
actually grant that new role

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.930
to our identity, and then we're
going to redeploy our function

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:37.780
with that identity.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:40.860
So now we've, again, gone from
this incredibly overprivileged

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.710
role down to exactly
the two permissions

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:48.370
that we need to read
from Vision and Storage

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:50.020
and then write to Firestore.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.770
And then one of my
other favorite things--

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:54.260
so Vision, you may have
noticed, doesn't actually

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:56.660
have an IM role.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.940
So it relies on OAuth scopes.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:02.240
And so you need this scope,
for instance, the Cloud Vision

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:02.957
scope.

00:16:02.957 --> 00:16:04.790
And so we'll give it
the Cloud Vision scope.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:06.050
And it doesn't
fit on the screen,

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:08.133
but we'll give it the Cloud
Storage scope as well.

00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:09.990
So it can go in right there.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:13.490
And we can use this cool thing
called the metadata server.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:20.210
So the metadata server
basically is an HTTP server

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:23.660
that kind of looks like it
lives on your compute instances.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.220
And what it will do is it
will fetch tokens for you.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:27.740
So when you deploy--

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:28.280
right?

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:31.332
How many people-- and be
totally frank with me--

00:16:31.332 --> 00:16:33.290
how many people have
uploaded a service account

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:34.790
to their compute?

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.770
Or like occasionally,
you upload it to GitHub

00:16:36.770 --> 00:16:37.820
because you screw up.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:38.390
I've done it.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:39.372
It's terrible, yeah.

00:16:39.372 --> 00:16:40.580
I see a couple hands on that.

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:42.890
Yeah, that's not great
development practice, right?

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:45.860
Because maybe your private
repo becomes public,

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:47.900
or somehow, those
credentials leak.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:49.460
The point of the
metadata service

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.668
is that you don't have to
ever deal with credentials.

00:16:51.668 --> 00:16:54.260
You can just say, hey, metadata
service, give me a token.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.540
And we refresh those
tokens every half hour

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.150
or an hour so you're not
touching credentials at all.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.710
You can just say
this is my identity.

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:02.180
Give me a token
for the identity.

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.680
And so that's what
we're doing here.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:05.390
And we're actually
even saying, give me

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:09.140
a token that scoped only to
the two or three services

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:10.250
that I need.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:12.619
And I can make that
request, get the token,

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:14.290
and then do my calls.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:16.069
And under the hood,
our client libraries

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:17.072
are doing this for you.

00:17:17.072 --> 00:17:18.530
I just want it to
be very explicit.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:21.410
Hey, this is a really cool
piece of functionality

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:25.448
that we provided
actually just recently.

00:17:25.448 --> 00:17:26.623
OK.

00:17:26.623 --> 00:17:28.040
So we've kind of
gone through half

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:30.680
of our flow, where we've been
able to securely upload a file

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:33.890
and then run some things as
admin securely before writing

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:36.050
back to Cloud Firestore.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:38.720
So the storage
rules that we wrote,

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:40.727
I would categorize
as pretty simplistic.

00:17:40.727 --> 00:17:42.560
If you've ever written
Cloud Firestore rules

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:43.620
they're a little trickier.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.703
So we're going to go through
some similar concepts

00:17:45.703 --> 00:17:49.290
and then see kind of how we
extend that into the future.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:52.070
So as I mentioned--
and this is going

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:53.990
to be kind of a slight
point of difference--

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.300
that write that we did
from the function happened

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:58.580
as an administrator,
which means that actually,

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.340
a bunch of these rules
are not enforced, right?

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:04.320
Because we go through IM, and
we do an IM check that says,

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:07.640
hey, your function is running
kind of as an administrator.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:08.840
Let's check there.

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:12.200
This is mostly the
Firebase rules are mostly

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:13.490
for your end users.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.790
And they're kind of designed
purposefully for that.

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.250
But I'm going to go through
an exercise of we also

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:22.580
want to ensure that maybe
there is a category of user who

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:24.920
is writing their expenses
directly to the database.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:28.790
Maybe we have a mode in our app
that you don't have a receipt,

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.040
and you just have to put
in the amount manually.

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:34.070
We still want to
provide for that.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.463
So we would have to kind
of write that schema out

00:18:36.463 --> 00:18:37.130
in our database.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.230
And also, it's
just good practice

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.340
to see people walk through this.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.910
So we'll start off with kind
of a similar set of constructs

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.120
that we had earlier, right?

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.110
You have a service.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:47.570
In this case, it's Firestore.

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:49.520
And you have that kind
of similar boilerplate

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:53.000
path of databases,
documents, et cetera.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.010
For those of you who
haven't used Firestore,

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:56.900
it's a document
collection database.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:00.080
So you have collections--
for instance, expenses--

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.530
and then a document
named the expense ID.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:06.460
So expense.jpg, or whatever.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.310
And we're going to say we
actually care a lot more

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:12.310
about schema validation,
so imagine we kind of did

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:13.900
the authentication
check, and we did

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:18.740
a if the user can do whatever.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:20.340
We want to validate,
for instance,

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:26.270
that unlike just the file
name, like we have in storage,

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.933
maybe our documents have
to have certain properties.

00:19:28.933 --> 00:19:31.100
So in this case, we're
actually going to write three

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.340
properties, the image URL--
so we could pull down our file

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:35.690
and display it in the app--

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:37.700
the amount of money,
and then the user

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:41.080
ID of the user that
actually wrote it.

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:42.540
And so the first
thing that we did

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:45.180
was we basically said
this document can only

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:46.140
have those properties.

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:48.360
That's what that
has only check is.

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:49.280
And the next thing
that we're going to do

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.870
is we're actually going
to type check those.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:52.250
We're going to
say, OK, the image

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:56.720
has to be a string because
it's just a URL out to storage.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:58.790
The amount has to be a number.

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:00.500
And the UID also
has to be a string.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:02.410
It's just a Firebase Auth UID.

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:04.710
So we've done just
simple schema validation,

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:06.710
but then we can do some
more complicated things.

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:08.840
We can say, for
instance, the image URL

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.070
has to match a certain
regular expression.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.760
So only allow them to store
images that are stored

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.950
in Cloud Storage for Firebase.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:20.143
Or maybe we don't allow receipt
expenses over $1,000, right?

00:20:20.143 --> 00:20:21.560
We're like, whoa,
you shouldn't be

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:22.860
doing that in our mobile app.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.310
So we're going to limit that.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.768
And the last one
that we want to do

00:20:25.768 --> 00:20:27.870
is kind of that quick
auth check of the user who

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:31.190
is writing this document has to
actually be that user, right?

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:33.020
I don't want to impersonate you.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:36.590
I don't want you changing my
document later, et cetera.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:39.240
And then if we wanted to,
kind of for completeness,

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:41.240
we could say things
like allow update, allow

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:42.920
delete, et cetera.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:46.453
If a rule is not specified,
everything fails closed, right?

00:20:46.453 --> 00:20:47.870
That's how this
whole thing works.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:48.495
So don't worry.

00:20:48.495 --> 00:20:50.870
Your entire database is
not public on the internet

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:54.290
unless you do something like,
say, allow reads and writes

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:56.420
everywhere, which
that is an option when

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:57.530
you start in test mode.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.210
You can say yes,
allow everything

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:00.918
to be public on the
internet because it's

00:21:00.918 --> 00:21:02.030
really fast to iterate.

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:04.488
Not great development practice,
and I'll get into some ways

00:21:04.488 --> 00:21:08.120
that we can help kind of make
that easier in the future.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:09.928
But there's another tool, right?

00:21:09.928 --> 00:21:11.720
If you're looking at
that and you're going,

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:15.830
oh god, I have model
classes with 15 or 20

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:18.590
different properties and I'm
storing them in Firestore,

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.430
this is going to get crazy
to do that all manually.

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:23.200
Do we have the tool for you?

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:25.732
So I always feel kind of funny.

00:21:25.732 --> 00:21:26.440
I work at Google.

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:28.180
I've worked at
Google for a while.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:30.240
Has anyone heard of
protocol buffers?

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:31.000
A couple of you?

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:31.270
OK.

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:32.050
It's a Google conference.

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:33.342
I guess it kind of makes sense.

00:21:33.342 --> 00:21:34.720
So protocol buffers
are basically

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:40.670
a way of defining a schema
independent of a programming

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:41.170
language.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.220
So it's an interface
definition language.

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:45.970
So I can define
an expense proto.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:49.420
And I can say, hey, that
has a string, image URL,

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:51.850
an amount of money, and a UID.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:53.200
Those fields are all required.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.750
Maybe actually I should
have made image URL optional

00:21:55.750 --> 00:21:58.713
because maybe we have people
typing in expenses manually.

00:21:58.713 --> 00:22:01.130
But you can actually generate
a bunch of things from that.

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:03.213
For instance, you can
generate all of your classes

00:22:03.213 --> 00:22:04.340
for your model objects.

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:07.980
So for instance, it would
create expense.java,

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:10.330
and it would create
expense.swift, and TypeScript,

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:12.490
and whatever other languages.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:14.380
And we've extended
it to also create

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:17.325
rules to validate those
model objects, right?

00:22:17.325 --> 00:22:18.700
So what happens
here is your flow

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:22.000
is you define your objects
and protocol buffers.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.642
And then it generates the
model objects and your client,

00:22:24.642 --> 00:22:27.100
as well as the rules on your
server to validate those model

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:27.950
objects.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.400
So if you're always deploying
those things in sync,

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:32.080
you should never
get to a point where

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:35.020
things are kind of egregiously
broken in your data model

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:37.460
because you should never
be writing those things.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:38.685
So definitely check that out.

00:22:38.685 --> 00:22:40.060
It's kind of an
open source tool.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:41.560
You can run it
yourself, and it will

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:44.710
generate all of the various
model objects that you want,

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.332
as well as rules
to validate them.

00:22:46.332 --> 00:22:48.040
And that makes it a
little easier, right?

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:50.350
Because now you just say,
oh, is this an expense?

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:51.550
And pass it in.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:54.570
And it's like yes or
no, it's an expense.

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:57.390
You still have to include
whatever kind of other business

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:58.980
logic checks you have.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:02.430
We're actually investigating
imagine if we go back

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:05.735
and instead of just saying
what they are, imagine

00:23:05.735 --> 00:23:07.860
if you could specify
conditions in a proto as well,

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:12.030
to say, hey, this value has
to be between 0 and $1,000

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:12.557
in amount.

00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:14.890
And then we could kind of
automatically do that as well.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:16.680
So there are some kind
of interesting ways

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:17.860
for improvement.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.140
The other one, just
kind of going on a limb,

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:20.973
is actually going in
the reverse and saying,

00:23:20.973 --> 00:23:21.900
you have a Java class.

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:24.360
Generate me rules off a
Java class to validate it.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:26.520
Well, that's a little
bit of a harder problem,

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.470
but still something
super interesting.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:31.230
And if anyone is looking for
fun computer science problems,

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.180
definitely build something.

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:33.900
Share it with us.

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:35.954
We'd love to see it.

00:23:35.954 --> 00:23:37.790
Cool.

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:40.910
So we talked through
that example of a user

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:42.970
writing some more complex data.

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:44.720
Now we know that our
users are going to be

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:47.318
reading from our information.

00:23:47.318 --> 00:23:48.610
So we'll do a similar exercise.

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:51.250
We'll say, great, users can
read all their own expenses.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.000
Everyone should know
what this looks like.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.150
We're just going to say, yep,
you can get a single document,

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:58.220
or you can query for
all of your documents,

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:02.180
assuming that your ID matches
the ID in that document, right?

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:03.830
Remember when we
created things, we

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:05.413
had to ensure that
those were matched.

00:24:05.413 --> 00:24:07.247
We're going to do the
same thing on queries.

00:24:07.247 --> 00:24:09.200
And so this would
prevent me from querying

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:12.667
someone else's data, which
obviously we don't want.

00:24:12.667 --> 00:24:14.500
And that's where we get
to the first gotcha.

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.875
And this is why Firestore is
a little interesting, right?

00:24:16.875 --> 00:24:19.300
So you can imagine that
we have in our expenses

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:21.460
collection three
different expenses.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.060
And maybe we have
more than that,

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:24.670
but we'll start with three.

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:26.140
And there are two
expenses by me,

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:30.160
user 12345, for $10 and $3.99.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:33.560
And then some other
user has $1.50 expense.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:38.380
So as you can see that and then
our other one is different.

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:40.450
And so you might kind
of natively expect that,

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:42.670
hey, I have this
set of rules that's

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:45.160
saying only give me my things.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:49.660
I'm in a query, and maybe I'm
going to get only my documents.

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:52.365
And that is not how
things work, right?

00:24:52.365 --> 00:24:53.740
People get really
tripped up here

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:58.690
because Firestore's like,
no, I can't feasibly give you

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:00.075
only those things, right?

00:25:00.075 --> 00:25:01.450
Maybe there are
only three items,

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:03.400
but maybe there are
three million items.

00:25:03.400 --> 00:25:05.817
And Firestore isn't going to
look at every single document

00:25:05.817 --> 00:25:09.280
to be like ah, yes, Mike
needs these three items.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:11.220
So you may kind of
reformulate that and be

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:13.020
like, oh, I'm in development.

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:14.820
All the documents
are my own, right?

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.130
I own every single one of them.

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:17.770
And that's the only
thing in the database.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:18.550
So you may do the same thing.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.467
You may say, hey, give
me all those documents.

00:25:20.467 --> 00:25:21.670
They're all mine, right?

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:24.702
And nope, Firestore
still can't do that.

00:25:24.702 --> 00:25:26.160
And again, kind of
what's happening

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:30.500
is we have to know kind
of beforehand if we

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:31.890
can fulfill the request.

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:32.390
Right?

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:34.830
So you actually
have to say, hey,

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:37.430
write a query that says give
me all of the documents where

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:39.470
the UID is equal to--

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:41.390
I hardcoded 12345.

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:43.700
But what I would do is I
would pull my UID client

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:46.580
side off that token
and then match that.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.230
And in that case, Firestore is
basically asking this question,

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:49.730
right?

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:53.540
Can I prove that this operation
will be allowed without ever

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:54.540
looking at the data?

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:55.040
Right?

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.140
Because if we had to look
at a billion documents

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:58.640
in order to return
you one document,

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:01.430
that would be an incredibly,
incredibly slow database,

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:02.750
an incredibly slow product.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.850
And that's not the
promise of Firebase

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.010
and especially not
Cloud Firestore,

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:10.280
where we have to do tens
or hundreds of milliseconds

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.570
of sync time.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:14.330
And so the answer in
that case is yes, we can,

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:16.580
because you've provided
information in the query

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:19.168
that we match with the
rules that you've written.

00:26:19.168 --> 00:26:20.960
So that's the concept
that you kind of need

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.360
to think about when you're
writing Firestore rules is

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:26.300
we are validating it based
on the rule and the query,

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:27.740
not based on the
underlying data.

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:31.220
OK.

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:33.557
So I said that I'd
come back to this.

00:26:33.557 --> 00:26:35.390
What about role based
access control, right?

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:38.030
So we've been living
in a world with--

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:39.845
we'll go-- oh, I won't
go all the way back.

00:26:39.845 --> 00:26:41.720
But we've been living
in a world with a bunch

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.550
of different attribute
checks, right?

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.700
Of ensure that the size is
less than this and the UID

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:48.230
matches this.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:49.910
But I think a lot of
people conceptually

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:53.330
think about things in role based
access control terms, right?

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:54.860
You're like, I'm a user.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.620
Maybe I have a
construction worker role.

00:26:57.620 --> 00:26:59.220
Therefore I can
access a tool box.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:26:59.720
Right?

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:02.120
Life just sort of
works this way.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:07.780
So RABAC as opposed to
ABAC, user role resource.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:09.423
And maybe there are cases.

00:27:09.423 --> 00:27:11.590
We've gotten a little more
fancy in our application.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:13.930
And we work for
a big enterprise.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.690
And they have admins who
are checking our expenses.

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:18.730
And we have to give
the admins access

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:21.620
to view our expenses
to audit or whatever.

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:24.700
And so we can write this
little kind of local function

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:28.630
and say, hey, if it's
me, allow me to read it,

00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:31.240
or if this person
accessing it is an admin.

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.570
But how do we get to that
kind of is admin check?

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:36.310
We can do that a couple ways.

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:38.470
But the simplest
is you can actually

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.130
write this kind of little
local function, is admin.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:42.880
And what it's going
to do is it's actually

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:46.130
going to look up data
that is in the database.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:49.240
So you can create access
control lists in your database.

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:52.210
Or for instance, if you're
building kind of like sharing

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.940
information, if you're building
a Google Docs or Google

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:57.010
Drive clone, you
could say, hey, these

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:59.352
are all the people who
have access to a document.

00:27:59.352 --> 00:28:01.810
In our case, we're just going
to create another collections

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:03.190
called admins.

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:05.770
And hey, we're going
to look up and say,

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:07.600
is this user in the
admins collection?

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.600
And if they're in the
admins collection, great.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.017
We'll allow them to be an
admin, which of course, brings

00:28:12.017 --> 00:28:14.350
this amazing interesting
question of like, oh, well, how

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:18.940
does someone get in the admins
collection if doing so now

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.170
grants them these
magical powers?

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:23.705
The zeroth order
answer is the one

00:28:23.705 --> 00:28:26.080
that I commonly do is literally
just go into the console,

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:28.580
and copy an ID, and paste it in.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.710
But that's obviously
not super scalable.

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:33.310
So let's imagine that
we're going to actually use

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:36.490
another piece of
functionality of Firebase

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:40.600
Auth in our integrations, which
are called custom token claims.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:43.180
So a custom claim
is something like we

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:46.030
have here of is admin flag.

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:49.960
And if we go all the way back to
that first section on Firebase

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:51.760
Auth, we talked
about that token.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.050
And we talked about the body.

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:54.675
And within that body,
there are a bunch

00:28:54.675 --> 00:28:57.400
of normal claims like my email.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:00.470
And we can augment those
with custom claims.

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:03.150
So we could, for instance, when
that token is being created,

00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:05.140
create it with this
additional flag that

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:07.420
says this person is an admin.

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:11.510
Therefore, they can do
another set of things.

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:14.050
So you might be like,
oh, man, that's awesome.

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:15.850
I can put all sorts
of information.

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:17.980
I can put the company
that they work at.

00:29:17.980 --> 00:29:19.930
You can put group information.

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:22.510
You can store profile photos.

00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:23.770
Don't do that actually.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:26.410
I had someone do a 10
megabyte profile photo.

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:30.280
Turns out HTTP headers do not
take well to 10 megabyte photos

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:31.660
in the header.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:34.480
And so we've kind of provided
a couple limits on that, right?

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:36.850
So custom claims aren't
kind of a panacea

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:39.700
for all of your
authentication needs, right?

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:41.350
We limit them to
a kilobyte so you

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:44.560
can only kind of put static
data and small amounts

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:45.910
of static data.

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:48.970
And also, we don't want them
to be heavily dynamic, right?

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:51.880
These things are refreshed
when the token gets

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:53.770
refreshed roughly once an hour.

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:56.410
And so don't store information
like the chat rooms

00:29:56.410 --> 00:29:58.690
that a user is allowed to
access because they'll still

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:00.480
be able to access it
for an hour, right?

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.647
If you need small
dynamic pieces of data,

00:30:02.647 --> 00:30:04.230
store that in something
like Firestore

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.060
in another collection, and
do a lookup like we had with

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:12.370
exists to actually look and
see if that document exists.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:13.790
Two shameless plugs.

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:16.750
So Jen Person, one
of the Firebase DAs,

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:19.750
did a really good talk that
dives all the way into use

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:22.570
cases around custom claims, when
you should use them and when

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:24.050
you shouldn't.

00:30:24.050 --> 00:30:29.210
And then if you enjoyed the kind
of data modeling perspective,

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:33.500
yours truly gave a absolutely
fantastic YouTube video--

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.280
yeah, not biased at all--

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:38.160
where we dive into kind
of more complex examples.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:40.220
And then we also solve
that bootstrapping problem

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.460
of how does the first
user get into a place.

00:30:46.292 --> 00:30:49.590
So we've written a
bunch of code thus far.

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:51.040
I don't know about you.

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:54.250
I know when I write my apps,
I don't always test them.

00:30:54.250 --> 00:30:56.580
So I'm not going to ask people
if they test their apps.

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.830
But it's something that we
all know that we should do,

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.570
like brushing our
teeth and flossing.

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:01.987
And we're going
to try and make it

00:31:01.987 --> 00:31:04.800
as easy as possible to
do those things, right?

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:06.450
So there are a couple
of ways that we

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.288
provide to actually test that
functionality out, right?

00:31:09.288 --> 00:31:11.580
I wrote a bunch of rules,
and you're like, great, Mike.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:14.860
Those look fine, but prove to
me that they're actually secure.

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:16.690
So that's what we're
going to do now.

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:19.620
So we provide the first
tool, which is a simulator.

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.660
And so a simulator
is in our console,

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:24.043
we allow you to write
where you edit those rules,

00:31:24.043 --> 00:31:25.710
actually test that
those rules are doing

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.990
what you think they're doing.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:30.660
So I've copied that
exact same set of rules.

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:32.590
Over on the left
side of the screen,

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:35.610
you can see, hey, we're
going to try a create.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:38.180
And we can create a document.

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:41.290
So I actually went to the
taco trucks across the street,

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:43.000
and so I don't have
an expense for it.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:46.490
But imagine that cost me $10,
and I took a photo of it.

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:49.270
So the photo is stored
over in storage, $10.

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:52.330
My UID-- remember
that number, 12345.

00:31:52.330 --> 00:31:52.870
Great.

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:54.370
I'm going to build
that document.

00:31:54.370 --> 00:31:56.495
I'm going to go down to
the authentication section,

00:31:56.495 --> 00:31:59.340
and I'm going to put
my UID in there, 12345.

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.180
And then I'm going to click Run.

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:05.410
And Run is going to say,
yep, your read or your create

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:10.240
to expenses slash lunch has
all those keys, matches,

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:13.040
is less than $1,000, et cetera.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:16.090
But if, for instance, I just
changed that Firebase UID

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:20.320
to 13579, so
basically not myself,

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:24.040
but I'm trying to write
a document that has that,

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:25.510
it's going to fail that write.

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.510
And that's obviously by design.

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:30.840
And the same would happen
if I say change the expense

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:32.820
to $2,000 or similar.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.280
It would kind of give you that
nice failure message of hey,

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:36.030
something's wrong.

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:38.160
So it's a really nice
way of doing that.

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.050
If I drop the
simulator down, it'll

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.090
also provide a total
version history, right?

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:44.433
So every time I save and
redeploy those rules,

00:32:44.433 --> 00:32:45.600
I'll have a version history.

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:47.315
So I can kind of
test that as I want.

00:32:47.315 --> 00:32:49.440
And then if something broke,
I could, for instance,

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.390
kind of one click
rollback in the simulator.

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:54.420
Hey, get back to a safe state.

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:57.540
But obviously, that's kind of
nice for these one off things.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:32:59.950
But ideally, when
you're developing,

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:00.990
you have a little bit--

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:03.420
you have unit tests or
an integration test.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:06.240
So we also provide
a local emulator.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:08.430
And so the local
emulator will basically

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:11.860
allow you to programmatically
do those similar tests.

00:33:11.860 --> 00:33:12.932
So you'd have your rules.

00:33:12.932 --> 00:33:14.640
And you have kind of
your local database.

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:16.020
You can read and write to it.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.160
And I can create--

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.560
hey, in my expenses
collection, maybe I

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:22.350
want to go and fetch your
expense versus my expense.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:24.780
And I've written rules
that kind of do that.

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:27.250
And then I can write
assertions and say, hey,

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:30.230
this test should fail under
this set of circumstances.

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:31.980
But maybe this test
should succeed.

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:33.420
And then I can validate those.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.190
So that's a really
nice way of, again,

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:40.360
getting more towards the
automation of your security.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:41.020
Awesome.

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:46.100
So we've talked a lot about the
deep technical aspects of how

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:47.675
to secure your application.

00:33:47.675 --> 00:33:49.550
But there are a lot of
things that actually I

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:53.150
think are more common
and more frequently done

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:56.760
incorrectly that are much
bigger security vulnerabilities.

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:59.510
So I'm doing another
talk tomorrow--

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:01.760
shameless plug number
three, I guess--

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:03.920
that goes really in-depth
on kind of threat models

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:05.090
and threat environments.

00:34:05.090 --> 00:34:07.100
But basically the
idea is there are

00:34:07.100 --> 00:34:10.070
threats in the world, everything
from the random people

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:13.310
on the internet pinging your
website to state sponsored

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:19.010
entities who are doing very
targeted and specific attacks.

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:20.880
And so in the category
of threats, right,

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:23.090
when you build an
application, you

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:24.710
want to kind of pick
the threats that

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:27.412
are most likely because
those are obviously--

00:34:27.412 --> 00:34:28.620
they're more likely to occur.

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.078
Therefore, you should kind of
try and solve them initially.

00:34:31.078 --> 00:34:33.114
Unless-- again, it depends
on what you're doing.

00:34:33.114 --> 00:34:34.489
But generally,
understanding kind

00:34:34.489 --> 00:34:37.370
of what threats are
out there, what tools

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:41.810
may be used to break
your application,

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:44.690
or have malicious things.

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:47.139
One of the most common ones--

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:48.860
and I think we call
it sort of insider

00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:52.460
risk or unintentionally
malicious behavior--

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:56.260
is everyone is really worried
about this guy, right?

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.510
The hacker who's sitting around
trying to break into your app.

00:34:59.510 --> 00:35:04.190
But practically speaking,
it's like this guy from IT

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:06.110
who accidentally
deleted everything.

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:10.400
That's a much bigger threat
than this thing, right?

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.490
Much lower probability, maybe
a little more dangerous,

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:15.050
but practically
speaking, if this

00:35:15.050 --> 00:35:16.820
is going to happen
98% of the time

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:18.680
because they clicked
the wrong button,

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:20.330
that is still a
really big threat

00:35:20.330 --> 00:35:22.390
that you want to deal with.

00:35:22.390 --> 00:35:25.800
So this
mike@mcdonald.com guy, we

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:27.330
don't know if we can trust him.

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:30.000
I wanted to add
him to my project.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.765
And typically, my alter
ego is an editor, right?

00:35:35.765 --> 00:35:36.390
Or is an owner.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.250
I can do anything
in this project.

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:42.100
But maybe I don't really
trust this other one.

00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:45.045
And so I want to give him kind
of more specific permissions.

00:35:45.045 --> 00:35:46.920
I don't want to give
him full access to, say,

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:49.845
all of my business
data or access

00:35:49.845 --> 00:35:50.970
to send push notifications.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.100
I want to say, hey,
Mike, I'm going

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:54.930
to give you a particular role.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:56.740
And we can actually
go and say, OK, there

00:35:56.740 --> 00:36:00.370
are some analytics roles
that are reader or writer.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:01.710
There's maybe a develop role.

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:04.168
And Mike is going to be doing
some development work for me,

00:36:04.168 --> 00:36:06.060
so I'll give him the
developer admin role.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:08.790
So he can go and delete
the entire database,

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:10.290
but he can't send
push notifications

00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:11.940
to spam all of my users, right?

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:14.740
So there is some
guide rails there.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:17.110
And if you need, you can
kind of bump out of that

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:20.320
into Google Cloud Platform and
use the custom roles in Google

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:23.440
Cloud Platform to create similar
to what we did for our IM

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:28.810
example, give mike@mcdonald.com
only access to a database

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:31.510
and maybe even only read
access to a database

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:32.963
and write access to storage.

00:36:32.963 --> 00:36:34.630
You can kind of create
whatever you want

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:37.290
and give them that custom
role, and the Firebase console

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:38.040
will respect that.

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:44.400
And the last one, again, kind
of going a little more abstract,

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:47.000
but developer experience is a
really, really important part

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:48.160
of security, right?

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:51.440
Because if your processes
are really cumbersome, right,

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:54.110
if you have a bunch
of manual steps,

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:57.760
people are going to kind of
bypass them or take shortcuts

00:36:57.760 --> 00:37:00.260
and end up in places where you
kind of unintentionally built

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:02.538
vulnerabilities into
your application.

00:37:02.538 --> 00:37:04.080
And there are a
couple of common ones

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:06.332
that we see really,
really often.

00:37:06.332 --> 00:37:08.040
Shared environments
is a huge one, right?

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:11.190
So for those of you who are
running Firebase apps today,

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:14.130
you may have things like
your dev, and your beta,

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:16.600
and your prod apps all
in the same project.

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:18.600
And that means they're
sharing an auth database.

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:21.180
That means they're sharing
an actual database.

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:25.050
That means your metrics are
kind of looking interesting.

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:27.780
Projects are kind of a nice
firewall between those, right?

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:29.970
Because you want an
entirely separate database

00:37:29.970 --> 00:37:33.450
that if someone comes along
and accidentally deletes it,

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:34.350
that's fine, right?

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:35.550
It was staging.

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:37.170
You can rebuild it.

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:40.270
And so really, really
recommend that.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:43.500
And I think there are a lot of
people who say, oh man, it's

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:45.270
really hard to set
up environments

00:37:45.270 --> 00:37:48.390
because it requires a lot
of manual toil of enabling

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:50.970
all of your APIs, et cetera.

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:54.390
So tools like Terraform
or Deployment Manager

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:56.590
will help you kind of
build that infrastructure.

00:37:56.590 --> 00:37:58.500
So the infrastructure
as a code type thing

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:00.780
will help you do that easier.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:03.660
And really into that, right,
if you have a very manual tool

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:08.340
chain, a lot of times, people
will do things like they have,

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:10.500
for building an iOS app,
have the one Mac Mini

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:13.080
in the corner of the office,
where they build the app on.

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:16.110
And then put a thumbdrive in,
and take the IPA file off,

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:17.610
and walk it over
to another computer

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:19.910
to upload it to iTunes Connect.

00:38:19.910 --> 00:38:21.660
That's a really bad
experience, and you're

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:25.870
likely to copy the wrong file
or do something like that.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.747
And so, CD tools, so
using Cloud Build-- or we

00:38:28.747 --> 00:38:30.330
have a really great
mobile tool called

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:33.480
Fast Lane, which does things
like automate provisioning

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:35.640
profile signing, and
upload to the app store,

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:39.240
and kind of doing that securely
so not everyone at your company

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:41.430
can say upload an
APK to the app store.

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:44.670
Only the Cloud build
deployment agent can do that.

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:48.810
You can kind of build some
additional security in there.

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:49.310
OK.

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:50.227
So that is all I have.

00:38:50.227 --> 00:38:51.580
I have about 10 minutes.

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:54.230
If you have questions, I
am happy to answer them.

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:57.510
And I will stick
around after as well.

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:00.080
Otherwise, please
submit your feedback.

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:03.260
And if you have other questions,
I'm on Twitter, @asciimike,

00:39:03.260 --> 00:39:07.430
A-S-C-I-I-M-I-K-E, and would
love to help you wherever you

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:08.900
are in your development journey.

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:10.970
Also, if you've built
awesome Firebase apps,

00:39:10.970 --> 00:39:11.940
send them my way.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:13.730
Send them to
Firebase on Twitter.

00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:14.810
Write into support.

00:39:14.810 --> 00:39:16.710
Again, I love seeing
what you build,

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:18.140
and I may try and break it.

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:19.597
And give you feedback on that.

00:39:19.597 --> 00:39:21.180
So thank you all
very much for coming,

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:22.305
and enjoy the rest of Next.

00:39:22.305 --> 00:39:26.240
